1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,200
Your memories, made into film. And sealed, so anyone can prove
it is real. This is Cinemory. It began as an anniversary gift.

2
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:25,100
Here it is running, live on Cloud Run. I add two photos from an
anniversary dinner. They were generated by a model, so nobody
real appears in this demo. The order I drop them in becomes the
order of the edit.

3
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:36,367
Then I pick an occasion. Each one brings its own score, its own
pacing and its own titles. Anniversary runs warm strings at
three and a half seconds a clip. Then I hit generate.

4
00:00:36,367 --> 00:00:50,600
Now the real work. Each photo goes to Kling on GMI Cloud through
Genblaze and comes back as a moving clip. That is a live model
call, so it takes about five minutes a photo. The job runs in
the background and the page polls it.

5
00:00:50,600 --> 00:01:00,200
And the reel plays. Those are real generated clips, animated
from the two photos I uploaded, and stitched into one film with
the occasion's pacing.

6
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:20,100
Now the part that matters. The provenance panel lists every
step, the model, the prompt, and a hash for each asset. I press
Verify, and the browser recomputes the SHA-256 itself. It
matches the sealed manifest hash, so the seal reads Verified.

7
00:01:20,100 --> 00:01:31,467
Underneath, every artifact sits in Backblaze B2 at its own
content hash, and each clip cites the exact source photo it came
from. Change one byte and the seal breaks.

8
00:01:31,467 --> 00:01:52,867
The design is one clean core with three ports. Genblaze drives
Kling and seedance through GMI Cloud. Backblaze B2 holds every
artifact behind a queryable index. FastAPI and React run on
Cloud Run. And offline fakes run the whole pipeline in tests,
with no credentials.

9
00:01:52,867 --> 00:02:04,400
It is live, open source, and when a model is down it degrades in
the open instead of faking a result. Cinemory. Memories, made
into film, that you can trust.
